    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d372-a4">

                <pb facs="0536" n="440" sameAs="frus:frus1898/pg440"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3 in No. 488.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Tokyo, Japan</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1897-04-17"><hi rendition="#italic">April 17,
                        1897</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your communication of date the
                    8th instant, relative to the bill lately passed by the Japanese Diet providing
                    for the payment of a bounty to the Japanese exporters of raw silk, which you
                    addressed to me as “doyen” of the diplomatic corps, I beg to state that the
                    foreign representatives do not consider that it would be expedient, under
                    existing treaties, for them, as a body, to make any representation whatever to
                    His Imperial Japanese Majesty’s Government in the matter.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <list>
                            <item>
                                <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edwin Dunn.</hi>
                            </item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. B. Walter</hi>, Esq.,<lb/><hi
                                    rendition="#italic">Chairman of the Committee of the Yokohama
                                    General Chamber of Commerce.</hi></item>
                        </list>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
